ing up too soon  and they're assuming well you got to  deceive yourself if you want to be happy  or motivated  um and so one thing that i spend a lot  of time on in the book  is pointing out that you know oh you  don't  like let's actually take an example from  carol tavis's book  because you mentioned her earlier in the  conversation and  and towards the end of that book uh  mistakes were made but not by me  which is all about like self-justifying  behavior  uh she and her co-author kind of  have this fatalistic attitude of well  you know you can't  we have to self-justify because if we  didn't  if we didn't convince ourselves that we  had made all the right choices in the  past then we would be consumed by  uh regret and guilt and you know  and depression and i read that and i was  like  wait are those our only two options do  we really have to choose between  like convincing ourselves against the  evidence that we've made all the right  choices in the past  versus you know being depressed and  miserable  like surely there's a third option here  which is to  find a way to be okay with the fact that  we've made mistakes in the past and  still be happy despite that  like and i think this third way exists  in basically all of the cases that  people have presented to me  where they say you know you have this  choice between being happy and motivated  or being rational  um i think there's there's always a way  to  make p to a very at the very least make  peace with your situation that doesn't  require you to tell yourself false  things  i think that a place where this comes up  in my own experience with scientists  is i've known scientists really super  duper good scientists  who will explicitly say that the best  strategy  for let's say a theoretical physicist is  to be their  own ideas biggest advocates  because you know let everyone put  forward the best possible  uh view of their own perspectives and  the free market will sort everything out  uh whereas i tend to think that the best  that the  the good theoretical physicists should  be their own ideas biggest critics  so their most obvious you know their  most you know they put forward like  here's all the reasons why this could be  wrong but i believe it anyway as we were  discussing  before and i suspect that the ones who  say well i should be my own theories  biggest advocates  are are sort of cheating in the way that  you just to pinpoint it like they just  kind of enjoy being their own theories  biggest advocates  rather than thinking that's the most  rational thing to do well  you know they it can be a good strategy  if your goal is not  to like come up with a true  theory and have that theory be accepted  if your goal is just  to get a lot of attention or to get um  i don't know prestige or but i think  that's not true i think that  let's give them the credit that they're  actually trying to find the truth  because i think that is  true these are people who will change  their minds like just because they're  their own theory's biggest advocates  today  doesn't mean they're at all consistent  with what they were saying last year  right right okay so so really the this  question is just  should we want like setting aside one's  own selfish  you know goals for attention or prestige  or whatever should we want other people  to be their own biggest their ideas own  biggest advocates is that how we  want science to work uh and  in theory that could work if everyone  was uh  if everyone was sort of you know playing  the role of an ideal jury  and they were hearing the best possible  arguments on both sides for the ideas  but in practice that doesn't actually  seem to be the way it works  it seems like very few people are taking  the time to delve into the  the nitty-gritty of the arguments for  and against the theories and the  the i mean i'm speaking mostly about  social science here because i'm more  familiar with that than with physics  so maybe it's a little different than  physics but um  but uh it's not in practice like ideas  get  they get attention and they get  publications and they get ted talks and  articles um without all that much  vetting of  whether they're really sound and so if  you have people who are just  advocating for their ideas without  worrying too much about whether those  ideas are true  i don't see that process really weeding  out the true from the false ideas very  well  actually maybe this is an interesting  distinction between social science and  physics is that  i think in physics it's maybe clearer  more often  whether an idea is true or false like  you can do pretty conclusive tests  oftentimes and so if you have someone  really pushing hard for an idea  and then it fails in experiments um then  maybe that's just kind of clear cut and  the matter is settled  whereas in social science the  experiments are so rarely clear-cut that  you like ideas rise and fall much more  based on the advocacy of  their proponents than based on the facts  from the world  and so the problem i suspect that the  rough life cycle  is very very similar in both cases but  the time scales are much quicker  in physics because like you say there  can be very definitive evidence you go  all right i guess i will move on  or even a definitive theoretical  argument um whereas in social science  not to mention the humanities uh you can  cling  to your increasingly less likely  position for a very long time without it  going quite to zero  right yeah yeah i i guess the time  scales maybe that's what i'm  uh what i'm pointing at although at the  end of the day i don't know  the quality of the evidence that we can  get even in the best worlds  um or the best possible cases in  like psychology yeah i don't know  i feel like you can do a bunch of  experiments and kind of get whatever  answer you want  um with a little statistical wizardry  uh on either side so i'm not even sure  that like long time scales would help  the problem in social sciences if if you  have everyone just being  a soldier for their ideas exactly i'm  not sure that that process would weed  out the two ideas from false even in  long time skills  that's right so that speaks to the idea  that individuals not just communities  should be tough on their own ideas which  i think is ideally yeah  i think so i think it's a better way to  go and you know just to  sum it up you you you do want to make a  case for the emotional rewards of being  like this it's not just an intellectual  reward right it's a satisfying way to  live  i do yeah i mean i think you know i'm  not going to deny that it can be  comforting to tell yourself  you know false but convenient things but  it is you know i and  and many people i know find it very  freeing  to be able to follow the evidence where  it leads without  feeling like you have to believe such  and such in order to be happy or in  order to  you know feel good about yourself or be  motivated  um that i think it's very liberating to  not feel like you have to believe  something in order to  you know get what you want um and  you know there's lots of rewards of the  joy of curiosity and figuring things out  that i think  come from scout mindset um eve you know  regardless of of the uh of whether the  conclusion is something that you had  wanted to be true there's kind of a  a thrill of of seeing the world more  clearly than you had before  um and so you know as always i'm trying  to be like reasonable and nuanced and  i'm not gonna claim that soldier mindset  has no benefits but  i think the benefits of scout mindset  are underappreciated and that if more  people appreciated those benefits  um then they would they would like find  my mindset  much more doable than they think it is  it sounds very good to me but i promise  that if contrary evidence comes along i  will change my mind about that  so i would ask for nothing less from you  sean julia gallo thanks so much for  being on the weinstein  it was so mu